---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';

const title = 'Privacy Policy — GPS-Go';
const description = 'Privacy policy for GPS-Go (com.yanerapps.gpsgo), a no-root Android location simulator by Yaner Apps.';
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} currentPath="/privacy/">
  <div class="prose">
    <div class="prose-header">
      <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
      <p class="lede">GPS-Go for Android (<code>com.yanerapps.gpsgo</code>)</p>
      <div class="meta">
        <span>Effective: 31 Aug 2026</span>
        <span>&middot;</span>
        <span>Updated: 25 Sep 2026</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p>
      This policy explains what GPS-Go does with your data. GPS-Go is developed by Yaner Yang, an individual
      developer publishing as Yaner Apps ("we", "us"). It applies to the GPS-Go Android application and to
      this website.
    </p>

    <div class="card">
      <p><strong>In short:</strong> GPS-Go has no user accounts and no servers of our own. Your simulated
      locations, history, saved places, tags, and routes stay on your device. Three third parties receive
      data when you use specific features: Google Maps and Google Places (map display and place search),
      Geoapify (place search), and Google AdMob (advertising). We never sell your personal information.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>1. Data that stays on your device</h2>
    <p>
      The following is stored locally in the app's private storage and is never transmitted to us &mdash; we
      operate no server and have no way to read it:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Simulated coordinates, custom names, favourites, and location history</li>
      <li>Tags, saved routes, and records of routes you have run</li>
      <li>Your local search history</li>
      <li>App settings and preferences (units, theme, joystick style, and so on)</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      You can delete this data inside the app at any time. Uninstalling GPS-Go removes all of it.
    </p>

    <h2>2. Your device's real location</h2>
    <p>
      GPS-Go requests location permission so it can show where you actually are on the map (the "Locate"
      button) and so it can register itself as a mock location provider. We never receive your real
      location, and the app never sends it anywhere on its own. One exception follows from how search
      works: when you search for a place, the centre of the visible map is sent with the query to rank
      nearby results, and if the map is following your position, that point is your real location
      &mdash; see section 3.
    </p>

    <h2>3. Data sent to third parties</h2>
    <p>
      GPS-Go transmits data off your device only in the situations below. Each is tied to a feature you
      actively use.
    </p>

    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Recipient</th>
            <th>What is sent</th>
            <th>Why</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Google Maps Platform</td>
            <td>Map viewport coordinates and zoom level, your IP address, device and SDK information, a pseudonymous SDK identifier, map interaction events, and crash diagnostics from the Maps SDK</td>
            <td>To render the map; Google also uses the diagnostics to keep the service working</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Google Places</td>
            <td>Your search text, the centre of the visible map (to bias results), your IP address, device and SDK information, and crash diagnostics from the Places SDK</td>
            <td>To return place suggestions and their coordinates</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Geoapify (OpenStreetMap data)</td>
            <td>Your search text, the centre of the visible map (to bias results), and your IP address</td>
            <td>To return place suggestions and their coordinates</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Google AdMob and the Google User Messaging Platform</td>
            <td>Your advertising ID (AAID) and app set ID, your IP address (from which Google may estimate a general location), device and app information, ad interactions, SDK diagnostics, and your consent choices</td>
            <td>To request and display rewarded video, interstitial, and banner ads, and to record your consent</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>
      Place searches are routed to Geoapify where possible and fall back to Google Places. Neither provider
      receives your location history, saved places, or routes. Geoapify states that it generally keeps logs
      of successful requests for no longer than 24 hours.
    </p>
    <p>
      When a search result comes from Google Places, its coordinates are removed from your local search
      history 30 days after the search, as required by Google's terms. Results from Geoapify and
      coordinates you enter yourself are kept until you delete them.
    </p>

    <h2>4. Advertising</h2>
    <p>
      GPS-Go displays ads supplied by Google AdMob, including rewarded video ads, interstitial ads, and
      banner ads:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Rewarded video ads:</strong> Never forced on you; only play after you tap a button offering extra daily usage in exchange for watching an ad.</li>
      <li><strong>Interstitial ads:</strong> May appear only when you close the summary shown after a simulation session ends, at most four times a day and at least three minutes apart.</li>
      <li><strong>Banner ads:</strong> Displayed inline within certain app screens.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      No ads are ever shown in the floating joystick overlay or in the ongoing notification, and a running
      simulation is never interrupted by an ad.
    </p>
    <p>
      We use Google AdMob only. We do not use any other ad network or mediation layer, and we have not
      added any analytics or crash-reporting SDK of our own. The Google SDKs described in section 3 collect
      their own diagnostics, which we do not receive. We never send your location history, saved places, or routes to any advertising
      service, and we never attach location data to an ad request as a custom parameter.
    </p>

    <h3>Consent (EEA, UK, and Switzerland)</h3>
    <p>
      If you are in the European Economic Area, the United Kingdom, or Switzerland, GPS-Go asks for your
      consent before ads are requested, using Google's certified User Messaging Platform. You can review or
      change your choice at any time in the app under <strong>Settings &rarr; About &rarr; Privacy Options</strong>.
      If you decline, the app remains fully usable; Google may still show limited, non-personalised ads,
      and whenever no ad is available the ad-gated action is granted without one.
    </p>

    <h3>US state privacy laws</h3>
    <p>
      We do not sell your personal information, and we do not share it for cross-context behavioural
      advertising in exchange for money. Residents of US states with applicable privacy laws are shown a
      notice with an opt-out choice, delivered through the same Privacy Options screen.
    </p>

    <h3>Controlling the advertising ID</h3>
    <p>
      Android lets you reset or delete your advertising ID at any time under
      <strong>Settings &rarr; Privacy &rarr; Ads</strong> (the exact path varies by device). Deleting it stops
      personalised advertising system-wide.
    </p>
    <p>
      Google's handling of ad data is governed by the
      <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Google Privacy Policy</a>; see also
      <a href="https://support.google.com/admob/answer/6128543" rel="noopener" target="_blank">how Google uses data in AdMob</a>.
      Geoapify's handling is governed by the
      <a href="https://www.geoapify.com/privacy-policy/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Geoapify Privacy Policy</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>5. Backup export files</h2>
    <p>
      GPS-Go can export your data as a file to a location you choose. That file contains your location
      history, saved places, tags, and routes in plain form. It is created only when you ask for it, it is
      not uploaded anywhere, and once it leaves the app it is yours to protect. Be careful where you store
      or send it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Android's own cloud backup may include app data if you have enabled it on your device. That backup is
      managed by Google under your Google account, not by us.
    </p>

    <h2>6. What we do not do</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>No user accounts, sign-in, or profiles</li>
      <li>No servers operated by us, and therefore no copy of your data held by us</li>
      <li>No analytics or crash-reporting SDKs added by us</li>
      <li>No sale of personal information</li>
      <li>No tracking of other people &mdash; GPS-Go simulates only your own device's location</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>7. Children</h2>
    <p>
      GPS-Go is intended for adults. It is not directed to children, and we do not knowingly collect data
      from children. Its Google Play target audience is set to adults only.
    </p>

    <h2>8. Data retention and deletion</h2>
    <p>
      Because your data lives on your device, you control its lifetime. The app deletes history older than
      the retention window you configure, and every record can be deleted individually or in bulk from
      within the app. Uninstalling removes everything. There is nothing for us to delete on our side, since
      we hold nothing.
    </p>
    <p>
      Data held by Google or Geoapify as a result of the requests described in section 3 is subject to
      their own retention practices and their own policies, linked in section 4.
    </p>

    <h2>9. Security</h2>
    <p>
      All network requests made by the app use HTTPS. Local data is kept in the app's private storage,
      which other apps cannot read on a non-rooted device.
    </p>

    <h2>10. Additional information for the EEA, UK and Switzerland</h2>
    <p>
      This section gives the information required by the EU and UK General Data Protection Regulation and
      the Swiss Federal Act on Data Protection.
    </p>

    <h3>Controller</h3>
    <p>
      The controller for the processing described in this policy is Yaner Yang, publishing as Yaner Apps,
      reachable at <a href="mailto:yanerapps@gmail.com">yanerapps@gmail.com</a>. Google and Geoapify act as
      independent controllers for the data they receive, under their own policies linked in section 4.
    </p>

    <h3>Purposes and legal bases</h3>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Purpose</th>
            <th>Data</th>
            <th>Legal basis</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Showing the map and returning place search results</td>
            <td>Map viewport, search text, map centre, IP address, device and SDK information</td>
            <td>Necessary to provide the service you ask for (Art. 6(1)(b) GDPR)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Keeping the map and search services reliable</td>
            <td>SDK crash and usage diagnostics, pseudonymous SDK identifier</td>
            <td>Legitimate interests in a working, secure app (Art. 6(1)(f))</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Personalised ads, and storing or reading ad identifiers on your device</td>
            <td>Advertising ID, app set ID, IP address, ad interactions</td>
            <td>Your consent (Art. 6(1)(a)), which you can withdraw at any time in Privacy Options</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Non-personalised ads, ad measurement and fraud prevention</td>
            <td>IP address, device and app information, ad interactions</td>
            <td>Legitimate interests in funding a free app and preventing ad fraud (Art. 6(1)(f)), and your consent where the law requires it for storage on your device</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Answering your emails</td>
            <td>Your email address and message</td>
            <td>Legitimate interests in responding to you (Art. 6(1)(f)); we delete the correspondence once the matter is closed, and within 12 months at the latest</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Data that stays on your device (section 1) is not processed by us at all. You are not required to
      provide any personal data. Without a network connection the map and place search do not work, and if
      you decline consent you see only limited ads. We make no decisions based solely on automated
      processing that have legal or similarly significant effects on you; with your consent, Google may use
      ad data to personalise the ads you see.
    </p>

    <h3>International transfers</h3>
    <p>
      Geoapify processes requests in data centres in the EU. Google may process data in the United States
      and other countries, relying on the EU-U.S. Data Privacy Framework, its UK Extension and the
      Swiss-U.S. Data Privacy Framework, and on Standard Contractual Clauses where needed (see
      <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy/frameworks" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Google's data transfer frameworks</a>).
      We are based outside the EEA, but we receive none of the data described in section 3.
    </p>

    <h3>Your rights under the GDPR</h3>
    <p>
      You have the right to access, rectify and erase your personal data, to restrict or object to its
      processing, to data portability, and to withdraw consent at any time without affecting the
      lawfulness of processing before the withdrawal. You also have the right to lodge a complaint with a
      data protection supervisory authority, in particular in the country where you live or work; in the
      UK this is the Information Commissioner's Office, and in Switzerland the Federal Data Protection and
      Information Commissioner. Section 11 explains how to exercise these rights.
    </p>

    <h2>11. Your rights</h2>
    <p>
      Depending on where you live, you may have rights to access, correct, delete, or port your personal
      data, or to object to certain processing. Since GPS-Go stores your data only on your device, you can
      exercise these rights directly in the app. For anything related to advertising data held by Google,
      use the Privacy Options screen in the app and Google's own controls. If you need help, write to
      <a href="mailto:yanerapps@gmail.com">yanerapps@gmail.com</a> and we will respond within 30 days.
    </p>

    <h2>12. Changes to this policy</h2>
    <p>
      If this policy changes, the updated version is published on this page with a new "last updated" date.
      Material changes affecting how data is shared will also be noted in the app's release notes.
    </p>

    <h2>13. Contact</h2>
    <p>
      Yaner Yang (Yaner Apps) &mdash; <a href="mailto:yanerapps@gmail.com">yanerapps@gmail.com</a>
    </p>
  </div>
</Layout>
